    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • XPENG Reveals G9L at the 2026 Paris Motor Show, Dubbed As the Ultimate \"Large SUV for the AI Era\"\n2027 Chevrolet Colorado Order Guide Confirms ZR2 Desert Boss Edition's Return\n7 photos\nPhoto: Chevrolet / edited\nThe Desert Boss story continues for 2027, with Chevrolet indirectly confirming the return of the off-road package through the order guide for the Colorado. Listed under regular production option code PDK, the dealer-installed limited production option is exclusive to the ZR2 model.\nHighlights include Multimatic jounce control dampers, auxiliary lighting, a purposeful front bumper, Tech Bronze-finished recovery hooks, door sill plates, and fender decals. The most important of highlights obviously comes in the form of the Multimatic-supplied dampers, which feature nitrogen-charged hydraulic bump stops.\nThose bump stops are designed to prevent bottoming out during extreme off-road impacts. Unlike stops that simply cushion the final inch of suspension travel, hydraulic bump stops rely on nitrogen gas and hydraulic oil to progressively absorb kinetic energy. This hardware is also featured on the ZR2 Bison , which indirectly replaced the original Desert Boss.\nBy offering these dampers on the ZR2 Desert Boss Edition for 2027, the Chevrolet division gives prospective customers a way to add the Bison's biggest differentiator without being forced to buy the complementary American Expedition Vehicles equipment. Said overlap also explains two exclusions listed in the 2027 model year Colorado order guide.\nAs it happens, the Desert Boss treatment cannot be combined with the Bison (RPO code ULV) or the Midnight Edition (RPO code WJP). The Bison already has those clever dampers, and the Midnight Edition's visuals would clash with the Desert Boss graphics, lighting, and bronze recovery hooks.\nPhoto: Chevrolet Colorado ZR2 Desert Boss Edition customers are presented with the BPH off-road front bumper package and the PCL off-road lighting package, but alas, the newcomer is a bit leaner than what Chevrolet used to sell originally. As a brief refresher, the previous model used to ship with a bed-mounted sport bar, a safari bar up front, beadlock-capable wheels, roof-mounted auxiliary lighting, an underbody camera system, and unique graphics.\nThe Multimatic dampers shift the Desert Boss from a visual and accessory bundle toward a more focused suspension upgrade, and that matters a lot more off the beaten path. Instead of looking ready for Baja, the new one promises to be more composed when its suspension runs out of travel. Customers who fancy the old beadlock wheels or sport bar will have to look elsewhere.\nAll Colorados feature a familiar 8-speed automatic transmission, but under the hood, things have changed this model year. Known as L4B, the 2.7-liter turbo inline-four gasoline mill that replaces the L3B puts out 350 horsepower and 450 pound-feet (610 Newton-meters) of torque.\nProduction should kick off later this month, with all Colorados hailing from the Wentzville plant in the state of Missouri. As ever, the Colorado range splits into the WT, LT, Trail Boss, Z71, and ZR2.\nPricing for 2027 has yet to be finalized. Until Chevy updates the configurator, it's worth keeping in mind that $32,400 from the outset makes the 2026 model year Colorado one of the most affordable midsizers out there.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nEditor's note: 2023 model pictured in the gallery.\nChevrolet Colorado 2027 Chevrolet Colorado Chevrolet pickup truck off-road\nDownload: 2027 Chevrolet Colorado order guide (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Apple Maps Gets DCE Update As There's Basically No Reason To Dump Google Maps",
